# Summary
Introduced bug: the commit added concurrent peer-RPC checkpointing and starts accepting peer RPCs before startup checkpointing, but did not route the startup checkpoint calls through the same serialization helper.
This change moves the experimental Raft peer listener into its own thread and starts it before the initialization block finishes. Peer RPC workers now checkpoint the durable restart artifact after handling a peer stream, using a new mutex. However, the startup path still calls authority.store_durable_restart_artifact(...) directly and does not take that mutex. Since the durable artifact writer uses a deterministic temp path based only on the destination file name and process ID, a peer RPC checkpoint and a startup checkpoint in the same process can concurrently create/truncate/write/rename the same temp file. A local process that can access the peer Unix socket, or a configured peer during multi-node startup, can trigger this race. The likely impact is control-plane process termination from a failed checkpoint rename or a restart artifact that later fails validation, causing availability loss for the experimental Raft control plane.

# Resolution
Fixed by commit dcaa70c85b637900917bdc7721e5f1c80197f6ab
(https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/dcaa70c85b637900917bdc7721e5f1c80197f6ab).

The fix routes experimental Raft startup durable checkpoints through the same
`durable_checkpoint_lock` helper used by peer RPC checkpointing, so startup and
peer-RPC checkpoints cannot concurrently write the same deterministic temporary
artifact path. Regression coverage was added to prove a peer RPC response is not
written while the durable checkpoint lock is held.

